"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][26]={"university_biology_en:M26-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M26-L1 – Plant signalling networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Molecular Plant Sciences and Biotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L1<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M20&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M20-L1<\/a>: hormones, binding\nand signalling networks.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret plant signalling\npathways as feedback networks and quantitatively evaluate a simplified\nreceptor occupancy model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A plant does not respond to water deficit with one universal\nresponse. Guard cells, root tissues and growing leaves can process the\nsame hormonal signal differently. Outcomes depend on receptor abundance,\ncell state, temporal dynamics and other signals. Molecular concentration\nalone therefore cannot explain a complete phenotype.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"signalling-and-context\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Signalling and context<\/h3>\n<p>Abscisic acid (ABA) mediates responses to water deficit and\ninfluences seed dormancy, among other functions. In a simplified core\npathway, ABA binds PYR/PYL/RCAR receptors. The complex inhibits PP2C\nphosphatases, relieving their inhibition of SnRK2 kinases. Activated\nkinases can phosphorylate target proteins. In guard cells, such signals\ninfluence ion transport and hence osmotic potential and turgor. Reduced\nturgor can decrease stomatal aperture. This conserves water but can\nrestrict CO₂ uptake.<\/p>\n<p>Auxin influences cell elongation, organ formation and root\narchitecture, among other functions. Polar auxin transport can generate\nspatial concentration differences; receptor and transcriptional networks\ntranslate these into tissue-specific responses. ABA and auxin can\ninfluence each other at several levels. There is no context-independent\nrule that one hormone always promotes growth and the other always\ninhibits it.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"model-and-identifiability\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Model and identifiability<\/h3>\n<p>For one independent binding site at equilibrium, occupancy can be\nrepresented as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=L/(K_d+L)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes free ligand concentration and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe dissociation constant in the same unit. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=K_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nhalf the binding sites are occupied. However, a signal may become\nstronger or weaker despite identical occupancy: amplification,\nphosphatase activity, gene expression and feedback operate downstream of\nbinding. A reporter therefore does not automatically measure receptor\noccupancy. Time-resolved observations and independent measurements help\ntest a model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_135_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two saturation curves for different dissociation constants; fictional parameters.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two saturation curves for different\ndissociation constants; fictional parameters.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Two saturation curves for different dissociation\nconstants; fictional parameters.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional receptor has\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin normalized concentration units. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=1,2,6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=1/3,1/2,3/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTripling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom 2 to 6 raises occupancy only from 50 to 75%. In a second model with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\noccupancy at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis only 50%. The example illustrates saturation and binding affinity,\nnot actual ABA receptor parameters.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate occupancy for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_d=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why could reporter activity decline despite unchanged\noccupancy?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What trade-off arises when stomata close?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8/(2+8)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor 80%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A downstream component may be inhibited; receptor occupancy and\nsignalling response are distinct quantities.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Water loss decreases, but CO₂ uptake may also decline.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The model assumes equilibrium, free ligand concentration and\nindependent binding sites. Actual ABA receptors, co-receptors, signal\ntransport and temporal adaptation can be more complex. All values are\nfictional; no physiological response can be predicted from them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Signalling networks link perception, regulation and phenotype. Next\nwe consider exchange between plants and symbiotic microorganisms.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M26-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7072425/\">Auxin-Abscisic\nAcid Interactions in Plant Growth and Development (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · ABA\nperception and auxin–ABA crosstalk; indexed review · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M26-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M26-L2 – Plant–microbe interactions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Molecular Plant Sciences and Biotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M7&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M7-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L1<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L2<\/a>: symbiosis, mineral\ntransport and host relationships.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain nutrient exchange in\nmycorrhiza and assess context-dependent interactions using a factorial\nmodel.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Plant roots are part of a biologically active soil system.\nMicroorganisms influence nutrient availability, plant development and\nmaterial cycles. Whether a particular association benefits a plant\ndepends on costs and benefits under the relevant environmental\nconditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exchange-at-biological-interfaces\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exchange at biological\ninterfaces<\/h3>\n<p>In arbuscular mycorrhiza, fungi form a network of hyphae outside the\nroot and branched arbuscules within root cortical cells. The fungal\nstructure remains separated from plant cytoplasm by a plant-derived\nmembrane. Specific transport proteins enable nutrient transfer at this\ninterface. Fungal hyphae can explore soil regions less accessible to\nroots alone. The plant may receive phosphate and other minerals; the\nfungus receives photosynthetically derived carbon compounds including\nlipids.<\/p>\n<p>Not all plant species establish this type of symbiosis. Exchange\ndepends on the partners, developmental state and nutrient availability.\nA high fraction of colonized roots is neither a direct measure of\nphosphate flux nor proof of increased growth. Functional measurements\ntherefore complement observations of colonization alone.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"interaction-contrast\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Interaction contrast<\/h3>\n<p>A factorial data model distinguishes two factors, such as nutrient\nstatus and symbiosis status. For two groups per factor, the interaction\ncontrast is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>01<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mn>00<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=(Y_{11}-Y_{10})-(Y_{01}-Y_{00})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first subscript identifies nutrient availability (1 = low, 0 =\nhigh), the second symbiosis status (1 = with, 0 = without symbiosis). If\nthe symbiosis contrast is identical under both nutrient conditions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the additive scale. On a logarithmic scale, absence of interaction\ninstead implies constant ratios. The scale should therefore have a\nbiological justification.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_136_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Fictional biomass values of 4 and 7 g at low and 9 and 10 g at high nutrient availability.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Fictional biomass values of 4 and 7 g at\nlow and 9 and 10 g at high nutrient availability.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Fictional biomass values of 4 and 7 g at low and 9 and 10\ng at high nutrient availability.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional archive contains mean biomass values (g) for four groups.\nWith low nutrient availability, means without and with symbiosis are 4\nand 7 g; with high availability they are 9 and 10 g. Contrasts are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>7<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">7-4=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10-9=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng respectively. Defining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>I<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas the first minus the second contrast gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng. Relative changes are 75% and approximately 11.1%. Means alone are\ninsufficient for statistical inference; dispersion and independent\nexperimental units are missing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the interaction contrast for group pairs 5/8 g and 10/13\ng.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is root colonization not a direct measure of nutrient\ntransfer?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What data are required for a confidence interval of the\ncontrast?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>13<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(8-5)-(13-10)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ng.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Colonized area does not establish actual flux across the exchange\nmembrane.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Individual observations or suitable information on variability and\nsample sizes, together with the dependency structure of\nmeasurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The fictional data illustrate an additive interaction, not a real\ncausal finding. Observational data may be confounded by soil properties\nand plant species. The values imply neither a practical treatment\nprocedure nor an agricultural recommendation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Symbiotic interactions depend on context. Next we examine the\ntemporal control of plant developmental programmes.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M26-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5940448/\">Plant\nSignaling and Metabolic Pathways Enabling Arbuscular Mycorrhizal\nSymbiosis (2017)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Nutrient exchange during symbiosis; indexed\nreview · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M26-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M26-L3 – Plant development and gene regulation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Molecular Plant Sciences and Biotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M6&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M6-L6<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L3<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a>: gene regulation,\nhormones and pattern formation.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish photoperiodic and\nepigenetic developmental control and interpret regulatory interactions\nusing a simplified model.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>The transition from vegetative to reproductive growth must be\ncoordinated with environmental conditions and developmental state. The\nmodel plant Arabidopsis thaliana demonstrates how a temporary\nenvironmental influence can produce a longer-lasting change in gene\nactivity. Its mechanisms cannot automatically be generalized to all\nplant species.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"seasonal-signals-and-gene-regulation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Seasonal signals and gene\nregulation<\/h3>\n<p>Photoperiodism describes a response to the relative duration of light\nand dark phases. In Arabidopsis, the CONSTANS system integrates light\nsignals with the internal clock. Suitable temporal overlap promotes\nexpression of FLOWERING LOCUS T (FT) in leaves. FT protein can move to\nthe shoot apical meristem, where it promotes floral development together\nwith transcription factors.<\/p>\n<p>FLOWERING LOCUS C (FLC) represses the flowering programme in many\nArabidopsis genotypes. Prolonged cold can result in epigenetic\nrepression of FLC (vernalization). The altered chromatin state may\npersist during subsequent vegetative development. It enables a changed\nresponse to later environmental signals without requiring a change in\nDNA sequence. The vernalization response is normally reset in the next\ngeneration. Photoperiod and vernalization are distinct signals that can\nconverge at shared regulatory nodes.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"quantitative-modelling\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Quantitative modelling<\/h3>\n<p>A purely educational model uses a normalized photoperiodic activation\nsignal\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand repressor activity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLet relative output be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=A/(1+R)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis equation is not established biochemical kinetics of FT, but a\nmonotonic response to activation and repression. At fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases proportionally with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndecreases with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn observed output cannot uniquely identify both inputs. Several\ncombinations generate the same result.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_137_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Simplified scheme: photoperiod activates FT through CONSTANS, vernalization reduces FLC-mediated repression.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Simplified scheme: photoperiod activates\nFT through CONSTANS, vernalization reduces FLC-mediated\nrepression.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Simplified scheme: photoperiod activates FT through\nCONSTANS, vernalization reduces FLC-mediated repression.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>In the model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=0.8/4=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt unchanged activation and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases to 0.4. Another combination,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nalso gives 0.4. All quantities are dimensionless. The example\nillustrates convergence and non-identifiability; it does not predict\nactual flowering dates.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>F<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why may the same FT measurement reflect different regulatory\nstates?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does an epigenetic vernalization response differ from a DNA\nmutation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6/3=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Different combinations of activation, repression and other\ninfluences may generate the same output.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It changes the regulatory chromatin state without necessarily\nchanging DNA sequence.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The model omits temporal dynamics, spatial transport, thresholds and\nother flowering regulators. Vernalization and photoperiodic responses\ndiffer between plant species and genotypes. This illustration represents\na selected model organism, not a universal law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Plant development integrates genetic, epigenetic and seasonal\ninformation. The next lesson links such traits to applied plant\nresearch.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M26-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7747966/\">Genetic\nand Epigenetic Understanding of the Seasonal Timing of Flowering (online\n2019; journal issue 2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Vernalization and photoperiodic\nregulation of flowering; indexed review · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M26-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M26-L4 – Applied plant research<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Molecular Plant Sciences and Biotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M18&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M18-L2<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L5<\/a>: experimental design,\nfunctional genetics and field research.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Recognize\ngenotype-by-environment interactions in multi-environment data and\ndistinguish evidence from phenotyping, genetic markers and prediction\nmodels.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>In applied plant research, a trait is rarely equally beneficial under\nall conditions. A cultivar may produce high biomass at a wet site but\nperform worse under drought than another cultivar. Analysis must\ndistinguish genetic differences from site and year effects. Molecular\nfindings and field performance represent different levels of\nevidence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"phenotype-genotype-and-environment\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Phenotype, genotype and\nenvironment<\/h3>\n<p>Phenotyping records observable traits such as flowering time, leaf\narea or yield. Sensors and imaging can scale data collection but require\ncalibration, standardized metadata and checks for systematic errors.\nGenotyping identifies genetic variants; an association of a marker with\na trait does not establish its causal function. Linkage disequilibrium\nand population structure can produce associations that fail to transfer\nto other populations.<\/p>\n<p>A multi-environment trial records genotypes in several site–year\ncombinations. A simple model is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_{ij}=\\mu+G_i+E_j+(GE)_{ij}+\\varepsilon_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>μ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the overall mean,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe genotype effect,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe environmental effect,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(GE)_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe interaction term and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ε<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe residual. Real trials also include, for example, block effects and\nrepeated observations. Depending on the question, genotypes and\nenvironments are modelled as fixed or random effects. Stability does not\nnecessarily mean a high average.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"prediction-and-validation\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Prediction and validation<\/h3>\n<p>Genomic prediction uses many markers jointly to estimate genetic\nvalues. Testing on randomly selected records can be overly optimistic if\nclosely related individuals or measurements from the same site–year\noccur in both training and test data. Validation using entirely withheld\nsites, years or families answers a different and often more demanding\nquestion. The target environment must be defined before evaluation.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_138_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Reaction norms of fictional genotypes A (8, 4) and B (6, 6) with reversed rankings.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Reaction norms of fictional genotypes A\n(8, 4) and B (6, 6) with reversed rankings.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Reaction norms of fictional genotypes A (8, 4) and B (6,\n6) with reversed rankings.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional cultivar comparison gives genotype A yields of 8 and 4\nt/ha in environments 1 and 2, and B yields of 6 and 6 t/ha. Both have an\nunweighted mean of 6 t/ha. A leads by 2 t/ha in environment 1 and trails\nby 2 t/ha in environment 2. The interaction contrast is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2-(-2)=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nt/ha. With equal weighting the means do not differ, but the ranking\nreverses. A mean alone conceals this crossover interaction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the means of A and B if environment 1 has a weight of 75%\nand environment 2 a weight of 25%.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What distinguishes a marker association from causal evidence?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why should validation for new site–years withhold entire\nsite–years?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.75<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.75\\cdot8+0.25\\cdot4=7<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nt/ha; B: 6 t/ha.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">An association may arise through linkage or population structure;\ncausal evidence requires additional independent support.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">This prevents correlated measurements from the same site–year from\ninflating estimated predictive performance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The values are simulated. No cultivar recommendations are justified\nwithout replication, measurement uncertainty and representative\nenvironments. Plant breeding and biotechnology also require assessment\nof ecological, legal and societal considerations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Applied plant research combines measurement quality, genetics and\nenvironmental context. The final elective lesson investigates modelling\nof yield responses.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M26-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7440334/\">Data\nsynthesis for crop variety evaluation. A review (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Genotype ×\nenvironment interactions and multi-environment trials; indexed review ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M26-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M26-L5 – Yield and stress models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M26 – Molecular Plant Sciences and Biotechnology<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master – elective profile<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M10&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M10-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L1<\/a> and <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M26&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M26-L4<\/a>: stress physiology,\nmodelling and genotype–environment interactions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply an empirical yield–water\nmodel, examine sensitivity to parameters and explain the limits of\ntransfer to new environmental conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Drought affects plants at several levels: water transport, stomata,\nphotosynthesis, development and reproduction. The relationship between\nwater deficit and yield therefore depends on stress timing and duration.\nA model can summarize part of these relationships without explicitly\nrepresenting every mechanism.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"empirical-yield-model\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Empirical yield model<\/h3>\n<p>Evapotranspiration (ET) combines evaporation from surfaces and plant\ntranspiration. A simplified empirical FAO relationship is:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-\\frac{Y_a}{Y_m}=K_y\\left(1-\\frac{ET_a}{ET_m}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Here\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis actual yield,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\na reference yield without the water stress under consideration,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nactual evapotranspiration and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nreference evapotranspiration without water deficit. Both ratios are\ndimensionless. The empirical factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes relative yield sensitivity to a relative ET deficit. It is not\nequivalent to water-use efficiency. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_a=ET_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe equation predicts\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_a=Y_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor the same deficit, a larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nimplies a larger yield reduction.<\/p>\n<h3 id=\"uncertainty-and-transfer\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Uncertainty and transfer<\/h3>\n<p>The model is a linear approximation over a limited validity range.\nVery severe deficits can mathematically produce negative yields, which\nare biologically meaningless. Such a result indicates invalid\nextrapolation, not a negative harvest. Temperature, nutrient status,\ndevelopmental stage and site conditions can alter the relationship.\nProcess-based models attempt to couple development, transport and carbon\nbalance explicitly. They require additional parameters and independent\nvalidation data. A more complex model is not more reliable merely\nbecause of its complexity.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_139_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Three linear model curves for relative yield loss at different fictional Ky values.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three linear model curves for relative\nyield loss at different fictional Ky values.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Three linear model curves for relative yield loss at\ndifferent fictional Ky values.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional reference yield is 8 t/ha,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_a/ET_m=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y=1.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRelative deficit is 0.2 and relative yield loss is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.2\\cdot0.2=0.24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.76<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_a/Y_m=0.76<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6.08<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_a=6.08<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nt/ha. Using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor 1.5 instead gives 6.56 and 5.60 t/ha respectively. This parameter\nsensitivity is not a statistical confidence interval because no\nparameter probability distribution was specified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate yield for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_m=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nt/ha,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_a/ET_m=0.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y=1.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the result at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ET_a/ET_m=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does parameter sensitivity alone not establish predictive\nperformance?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1.1<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8.9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10(1-1.1\\cdot0.1)=8.9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nt/ha.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>Y<\/mi><mi>m<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y_a=Y_m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nregardless of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Sensitivity shows dependence on parameters; predictive performance\nrequires assessment with independent observations.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The numbers are educational model data, not site- or\ncultivar-specific parameters. The model provides neither irrigation\ninstructions nor a reliable yield forecast. It omits other stressors and\nassumes comparable reference quantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Empirical yield models connect physiological processes with\nobservable consequences. This concludes the plant science elective\nprofile. The final research pathway brings together methods from\ndifferent profiles.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Further reading:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M27&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M27-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.fao.org/4/X5647E/x5647e0e.htm\">FAO – Annex II:\nIrrigation scheduling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Yield response to evapotranspiration\ndeficit; equation 1 · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7440334/\">Data\nsynthesis for crop variety evaluation. A review (2020)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Genotype ×\nenvironment interactions and multi-environment trials; indexed review ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
